        "regression_of": "F-61 (cycle 2)",
        "what_the_earlier_repair_fixed": "A keyboard-only operator scrolling up with the arrow keys kept follow true, so the next update scrolled back to the end and discarded the line they were reading.",
        "what_it_broke": "Pressing down or pagedown at the exact bottom now sets follow false, moves nothing on screen, prints no notice, and leaves every later token off-screen. The mouse path is deliberately asymmetric, so the gesture is safe with a pointer and unsafe without one — the exact inversion of the complaint F-61 was raised on.",
        "aim_at": "the unpin contract, not the key list: unpin only when the reading position actually moves away from the bottom. Both directions must hold simultaneously, and the accessibility lens's mutation probe — deleting TranscriptPane.on_key and watching the old behaviour return — is the proof the repair works."
      },
      {
        "fix_id": "fix-5fb6e79d5a8f",
        "child": "#108",
        "findings": [
          "F-86"
        ],
        "regression_of": "F-49 (cycle 2), itself a regression of finding 33 (cycle 1)",
        "what_the_earlier_repair_fixed": "F6 and F7 were dead from the composer focus the application launches in, because Textual's TextArea binds them to select-line and select-all.",
        "what_it_broke": "The repair did not make F6 and F7 work from launch focus; it added F11 and F12 as new collision-free keys and left F6 and F7 dead there. docs/terminal-ui.md was corrected; the running application's own command list was not, so it still tells the operator to press a key that does nothing.",
        "aim_at": "the key space and the focus contract, not a third key swap. This is the third cycle on one collision. Decide which keys reach the pickers from every focus, then make every statement of that contract agree — the in-app command list, the documentation table, and the code comment — and pin the agreement with a test that reads both surfaces."
      }
    ]
